  "articleSummary": "The article argues that hiring an AI engineer based on impressive demos or resumes is risky because the hardest parts of AI work—failure handling, permissions, and cost control—are invisible. The author advises hiring for the judgment behind the demo, making judgment observable through real work exercises and targeted questions about system behavior under uncertainty. Key recommendations include writing the job’s first-90-days outcome, asking candidates to walk through a project in depth, and probing specific scenarios like timeout recovery and transaction idempotency. The tone is analytical and practical, aimed at technical managers or founders, with recurring metaphors of “opening the hood” and “silence is an expensive answer.”",
